description:
F5 launches profile through `${config:java.profiler.args}` in every .vscode/launch.json entry, which the tracked .vscode/settings.json sets to `-agentpath:${workspaceFolder}/.profiler/libasyncProfiler=start,...`. `-agentpath` is fatal when that library is missing, and async-profiler has no Windows build, so F5 cannot start any scene on Windows ("Could not find agent library ... libasyncProfiler").

A generated `@.profiler/jvm.args` file was considered and rejected: VS Code's Java debugger writes vmArgs into its own @argfile, and the Java launcher does not expand an @file named inside another @file (tested: it takes it for the main class).

Instead the app loads the profiler itself. `tools.profiler:async-profiler` (Apache-2.0, Maven Central, 4.6) carries the `one.profiler.AsyncProfiler` Java API and bundles linux-x64, linux-arm64 and macOS libraries in the jar. At startup, desktop builds only:
- skip when an async-profiler agent is already loaded (`ixdar-cli run-scene`/`launch` pass their own `-agentpath`, which stays as it is);
- else load `.profiler/libasyncProfiler` when it exists, or the jar's bundled library;
- start cpu profiling, and on JVM exit dump `tmp/profiles/f5-<time>-<pid>.html`, the same name and directory the F5 setting used, so `profile_capture` and `profile-report` find it unchanged;
- on Windows, or any failure to load, log one line and run unprofiled.

The launch entries, `ixd wt launch-add`'s template (ai-workspace ixd/worktree.py) and the settings drop `${config:java.profiler.args}` / `java.profiler.off`. The web (TeaVM) build never references the profiler class.
